<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-14" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Multi-Variable Functions</title>
<introduction>
    <p>
      For the remaining of the quarter, we will be studying multi-variable functions <m>z=f(x,y)</m>. This section will serve as an introduction to multi-variable functions
      before looking at the calculus of multi-variable functions (derivatives and integals).
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
    <title>Multi-Variable Functions</title>
     <introduction>

    <p>
    In this section we will look at:
    </p>

  </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Graphing three-dimensional functions
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Contour maps and level curves
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Limits and continuity of multi-variable functions
        </p>
      </li>
                </ul>
  </objectives>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-3d-graphs">
    <title>Plots of Multi-Variable Functions</title>
    <p>
     The graph of a multi-variable is a surface that changes "height" (<m>z</m>-variable) over the <m>xy-</m>plane. This is difficult (at least for me) to draw 
     accurately by hand, so we turn to a digital tool to help us. You can use GeoGebra 3D, Desmos 3D or CalcPlot3D to do this. For each tool simply type right side of the 
     function into the input bar for the calculator. Here is an example of plots for the function <m>f(x,y)=e^{-x^2-y^2}-e^{-(1-x)^2-(1-y)^2}</m> from each of these tools.
    </p>
    <figure>
    <caption>GeoGebra 3D Example</caption>
      <image source="gg3dexample.png" width="100%">
        <description>
        <p>
        a three dimensional plot of e to the quantity negative x squared minus y squared minus e to the quantity minus one minus x squared minus one minus y squared
        using geo gebra 3d
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<figure>
    <caption>Desmos 3D Example</caption>
      <image source="desmos3dexample.png" width="100%">
        <description>
        <p>
        a three dimensional plot of e to the quantity negative x squared minus y squared minus e to the quantity minus one minus x squared minus one minus y squared
        using desmos 3d
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<figure>
    <caption>CalcPlot3D Example</caption>
      <image source="calcplot3dexample.png" width="100%">
        <description>
        <p>
        a three dimensional plot of e to the quantity negative x squared minus y squared minus e to the quantity minus one minus x squared minus one minus y squared
        using calc plot 3d
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>

  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-contour-maps">
    <title>Contour Maps</title>
    <p>
      Another type of plot that we can make is called a contour plot or topographical map. A level curve is a two-dimensional plot for a constant value of <m>z</m>. This 
  creates a slice of the surface parallel to the <m>xy-</m>plane. Collecting <m>c=f(x,y)</m> graphs for various constants forms a two-dimensional plot where the level curves
  show the contours or topography of the three-dimensional surface. Here is an example plot generated by Wolfram Alpha (the best tool for contour maps) for the function
  <m>f(x,y)=\cos(x)\cos(y)</m>.
    </p>
    <figure>
    <caption>An Example Contour Map</caption>
      <image source="wolframcontourexample.PNG" width="100%">
        <description>
        <p>
        a three dimensional plot of cosine x times cosine y with its related contour plot generated by wolfram alpha.
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<p>
  Wolfram Alpha colors this map so that peaks are lighter in color and valleys are darker. Contour maps may also be labeled with the "elevation" or constant of each level 
  curve like in this plot.
</p>
<figure>
    <caption>Another Contour Map</caption>
      <image source="contour-lines.png" width="100%">
        <description>
        <p>
        A contour plot of two hills with one hill labeled with elevations 10, 20, 30 and 40.
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<p>
  Here is a contour or topographic map of the Mauna Loa volcano in Hawaii. The contours or level curves are at increments at every 1000 feet as noted on the map. The scale 
  of the <m>xy</m>-plane is given at the bottom in kilometers.  
</p>
<figure>
    <caption>Mauna Loa Contour Map</caption>
      <image source="hawaii_contour0205.gif" width="100%">
        <description>
        <p>
        A contour map of the mauna loa explained in more detail below.
        </p>
        </description>
      </image>
</figure>
<p>
  From this contour we can note a few things. Concentric circles that have larger values as you move to the center represent a peak or maximum. Using the vocabulary
  you learned in Calculus I (Math 151), there are three local maximum values on this map. The main peak (Mauna Loa) near the center of the map with an elevation of over
  13,000 feet. Another local maximum and slightly shorter peak (Mauna Kea) is just above this on the map with an elevation of over 12,000 feet. A third unnamed peak or
  local maximum is in the top left of the map with an elevation over 5000 feet.
</p>
<p>
  Though not present on this map, a local minimum or valley would look similar but with decreasing values as you move to the center.
</p>
<p>
  A feature not possible on the two-dimensional plots you studied in Math 151 is a saddle point. This is part of the graph shaped liked a saddle and the point is the place
  where you would sit on the saddle. On this map, if you draw a line from the peaks at Mauna Loa and Mauna Kea, and then travel north along this line, you would descend from 
  Mauna Loa and then ascend to Mauna Kea where the approximate midpoint would be a minimum value between the peaks, But also at this point you would be at an elevation 
  of about 7000 feet and if the traveled from this point east or west, you would descend again. So at this saddle point. If you look north or south you are looking uphill
  to one of the peaks, but if you look east or west, you look downhill. So this is mixture of minimum point in one direction and a maximum in other directions.
</p>
<p>
  We will study maximum, minimum and saddle points further in a future section as an application of derivatives.
</p>
<p>
  Two other things that contour maps tell up about a derivative are the rate of change and the directional derivative. If we start one again at the top of the peak of 
  Mauna Loa and draw a line pointing to the right (east). If our line is 40 kilometers long, using the scale along the bottom, this line would extend from the peak at 
  Mauna Loa to a point near the contour at 3000 feet. Along this line we have lost about 10,000 feet in elevation over 40 kilometers giving an average
  rate of change of -10,000/40 = -250 feet per kilometer. If we travel along a 40-kilometer line south instead we descend to an elevation of 1000 feet, giving a rate 
  of change of -12,000/40 = -300 feet per kilometer. The line south has more contour lines closer together giving a steeper descent than the line east. How steep the
  descent or accent is measured be the derivative (instantaneous rate of change). This can be different in different directions. It is natural to look at the derivative
  in the <m>x</m> and <m>y-</m>directions, we can form a derivative in any direction. We will see how to do this in a future section.
</p>
<paragraphs>
