  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-3d-limits">
    <title>Limits and Continuity of Three-Dimensional Functions</title>
    <p>
     Before we look at more about derivatives, we will close this section looking at limits and continuity (and domain) for multi-valued functions. Just like for single-variable
     functions in Math 151, the multi-variable functions we will grow to know and love (exponential, logarithm, trigonometric, square root, etc.) are continuous on their 
     domains. Here are three examples of finding the domain of a multi-variable function.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find the domain of the function: <m>f(x,y)=4\ln(y^2-x)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the function inside a logarithm must be positive for the logarithm to be defined, we must have
        <md>
          y^2-x \gt 0 \to y^2 \gt x \to x \lt y^2
        </md>
        This defines a region in the <m>xy-</m>plane shaped like a parabola with a vertex at the origin and opening to the right. The domain is the region inside this
        parabola.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find the domain of the function: <m>f(x,y)=4x^2+y^2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the function does not include a logarithm, square root or denominator that would restrict the domain, the domain is the entire <m>xy-</m>plane. Mathematicians 
        shorthand this with <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> pronounced "artoo" like everyone's favorite droid from Star Wars.
        
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Find the domain of the function: <m>f(x,y)=\sqrt{x^2+y^2-4}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the function inside a square root must be non-negative, we must have
        <md>
          x^2+y^2-4 \geq 0 \to x^2+y^2 \geq 4
        </md>
        Since <m>x^2+y^2 = 4</m> is a circle in the <m>xy-</m>plane, the domain is the region on or outside the circle.
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Knowing the domain of our function is the same as knowing where a function is continuous for the functions that we will be studying. It can be more complicated
      for some special types of functions like piece-wise defined functions or other strange functions that mathematicians have dreamed up. We can formally define 
      continuity using the usual calculus definition that a function is continuos at a point <m>(a,b)</m> if 
      <md>
        \lim_{(x,y)\to (a,b)} f(x,y) = f(a,b)
      </md>
      Note that when we calculate limits for multi-variable functions, we take a limit for both <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> at the same time. This leads to a new possibility 
      to consider for limits that we will see in the final example below.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Calculate the limit
        <md>
          \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,2)} \frac{3x+y}{x^2+y^2+1}
        </md>
        Since this function has no restrictions on the domain since the denominator is always positive, we can calculate the limit by direct substitution.
        <md>
          \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,2)} \frac{3x+y}{x^2+y^2+1} = \frac{3(1)+2}{1^2+2^2+1} = \frac{5}{6}
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Calculate the limit
        <md>
          \lim_{(x,y)\to(3,0)} \frac{x^3\sin(y)}{y}
        </md>
        For this function <m>y\ne 0</m>. But we can rewrite this function as a product of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> functions
        <md>
          \lim_{(x,y)\to(3,0)} \frac{x^3\sin(y)}{y} = \left(\lim_{x \to 3} x^3\right)\left(\lim_{y \to 0} \frac{\sin(y)}{y}\right) = (27)(1) = 27
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
     <example>
      <p>
        Calculate the limit
        <md>
          \lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{x^2}{x^2+y^2}
        </md>
        This function is only undefined at the origin, which is also our limiting point. If we approach this limit from different directions, we will get different results.
        <md>
          \text{along the x-axis}\,\,(y=0) \quad \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{x^2}{x^2+0^2} = 1
        </md>
        <md>
          \text{along the y-axis}\,\,(0=0) \quad \lim_{y \to 0} \frac{0^2}{0^2+y^2} = 0
        </md>
        Along the different paths to the origin, we get different results. This is analogous to the right and left-hand limits not being equal in Calculus I. If we get
        different results from different "sides" then the limit does not exist! Try graphing this to explore what is happening at the origin for this function.
      </p>
    </example>
    <paragraphs>
    <title>Reading Check-in</title>
    <p>
      Check our WAMAP course page for a few questions that go along with today's reading on limits of multi-variable functions.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
   </subsection>
</section>